// smallcaps-demo — small caps two ways. Latin Modern and Alegreya carry a dedicated small-caps cut, drawn
// directly. EB Garamond has no separate small-caps file, but its regular cut carries the OpenType smcp
// feature, so the engine synthesizes small caps from its lowercase letters. Both go through \smallcaps.

\use{document}

\title{Small Capitals}
\author{The texish engine}
\date{Version 0.21.1}
\maketitle

\section{A drawn small-caps cut (Latin Modern)}

The body face here is Latin Modern, which ships a real small-caps font. \smallcaps{The quick brown fox jumps
over the lazy dog} — set with \verb|\smallcaps|, the lowercase letters come from the small-caps cut and the
capitals from the roman.

\section{Synthesized small caps (EB Garamond)}

{\font ebgaramond 12
EB Garamond has no separate small-caps file. Its roman does carry the OpenType \mono{smcp} feature, so
\smallcaps{the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog} is set by turning the lowercase letters into the font's
own small capitals — the capital letters stay full height and the rest drop to small caps. The \mono{smcp}
above is set in the document's typewriter face: EB Garamond has no monospace member, so \verb|\mono| falls
back to it rather than failing.
}

